foreign  [Music]  this is your Tech news briefing for  Friday November 18th I'm Zoe Thomas for  The Wall Street Journal  the collapse of FTX has caused pain for  customers and the crypto sector at large  it was also a massive blow for ftx's  roughly 300 employees many of whom held  a stake in the company or owned its  in-house cryptocurrency ftt The Wall  Street Journal spoke with more than a  dozen current and former employees many  of whom said they were stunned by ftx's  Swift demise and shocked by the alleged  misuse of customer funds by its founder  Sam bankman freed joining us to discuss  where this all leaves ftx's workers is  our reporter Alexander osipovic hi Alex  thanks for joining me thanks for having  me so you spoke with FTX staff what was  it like for them at the company before  the collapse it was a pretty good place  to work that they got paid well they  thought that they were part of this  idealistic Enterprise because Sam  bankman freed was so associated with  philanthropy and the movement called  effective altruism he pledged to give  one percent of the exchanges fee Revenue  to charity so these uh people thought  that they were doing good working at a  fast-growing company that was going to  change the world obviously things didn't  work out so great for the company but  how did employees of FTX find out about  the collapse and the allegations of  misuse of customer funds that have been  made the employees were basically  blindsided by everything that went down  on very very few of them seem to have  known about the misuse of customer funds  it appears the knowledge of that was  kept tightly within a small circle of  Executives the founders around Sam  makeman freed all the other employees  I've talked to said they didn't suspect  anything so they were utterly shocked  they basically found out that it was  happening the same time we and the  public did through tweets and media  reports and things like that and one of  the big complaints that the employees  had last week as FTX was trying to sell  itself and things like that they weren't  hearing anything from management Sam  came in with some belated updates over  the company slack Channel but they were  very frustrated they weren't hearing  anything sooner and can you tell us what  their reactions were as they did find  out about the collapse and some of the  actions that were taken by Sam bankman  freed and other Executives we have  sources who told us that one FTX  executive Ryan salami actually threw up  when he learned about the misuse of  customer funds uh because it was such a  shock to him there are other employees  who quit in fact I would say many  perhaps most of the staff quit over the  space of a few days they didn't want to  be associated with the FTX anymore  they've submitted their resignations uh  sort of a rush for the exits and many  people sent Sam angry messages or  expressed their discontent over chats or  even over Twitter in one case the  Fallout though for these workers it goes  beyond losing their job doesn't it  weren't a lot of them investors in FTX  as well yes this was a financially  ruinous for a lot of these employees  many of them had taken the opportunity  to buy FTX Equity there are different  opportunities of doing that and  sometimes they got it as bonuses and all  that Equity was wiped out in the  bankruptcy in addition to that some of  them actually were big holders of the  ftt token which is a cryptocurrency  created by FTX itself that has almost  completely collapsed in value during all  these events and lastly many of the  international employees actually were  paid by direct deposits to FTX so  instead of getting direct deposited to  the bank they got paid by deposits to  The Exchange ftx.com and so when ftx.com  froze to its customers the employees  also couldn't withdraw their money and  FTX has its headquarters in the Bahamas  is this the case both for the expats who  were working there and for the bahamians  who were employed by FTX as well well  the uh collapse of FTX had somewhat  different impacts on The Expatriate  employees in the Bahamas and the local  hires  the local hires were excited about this  new industry that was coming to their  Island and some of them you spent  thousands of dollars to buy FTX Equity  but generally they weren't in senior  roles they were in sort of logistics  compliance in some cases and they were  of course very disappointed by the  events that happened in the case of The  Expatriate employees these are usually  people who are fairly well off or  successful in their own right and they  essentially responded by leaving the  island some of them had trouble as they  were leaving for instance they had FTX  credit cards and the credit cards in  some cases were declined as they were  trying to fly out of the Bahamas to go  back to New York or Hong Kong or other  places you know a lot of customers  really put a ton of faith and trust in  Sam bankman freed he was seen as this  sort of good voice for the crypto  industry before this all happened what  was his reputation like inside the  company and you know Howard employees  feeling about that changing now so I  would say inside the company generally  people believe that he was a good guy  the sort of myth that you've seen about  him outside was true inside the company  they thought that he was driven by  ethics and philanthropy and he and his  sort of inner circle talked a lot about  effective altruism and maximizing the  value of the things that they were doing  there were some kind of quirks that I  think weren't really apparent to the  outside uh he wasn't always the best  manager he ran a very flat organization  and so as FTX grew he became kind of  this bottleneck and everybody needed to  get Sam's approval to do anything and  sometimes he was kind of unresponsive  and there was also  um I think some resentment of some of  the inner circle around him as having  too much power but on the whole I think  that people were generally pretty happy  to work there so what's the future then  for workers who have left FTX and for  those who've stayed with the company my  understanding is not that many people  have stayed with FTX there are a small  number of people who are helping to wind  down the business and help the new  leadership of the company that was put  in charge after the bankruptcy  proceedings began the people who have  left I think will have something of a  challenge because their refutations have  been tarnished even if they knew nothing  about the misuse of customer funds they  feel like they now have this big toxic  patch on their resume they're also  worried about potentially being targeted  in lawsuits and investigations and  things like that and are fearing that  that might consume their lives for the  next few years at least all right that  was our reporter Alexander ossipovic  thanks so much for joining us Alex  thanks very much for having me  and that's it for Tech news briefing  this week our producer is Julie Chang  our supervising producer is Chris  zinsley and I'm your host Zoe Thomas  thanks for listening and have a great  weekend  [Music]  [Music]

